WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.090
Handling Customer Concerns.

2
00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:07.692
You've framed the application to get
ahead of common concerns. But sometimes a

3
00:00:07.692 --> 00:00:13.003
prospect still pushes back. Here's how
the best AEs on our team handle it.

4
00:00:13.003 --> 00:00:17.275
Tactic one: validate, align, answer.

5
00:00:17.275 --> 00:00:23.025
When a prospect raises a concern, Andy
Murphy follows a three-step pattern.

6
00:00:23.025 --> 00:00:28.931
Listen to how he handles a question from
Guy Lelouch at GovPlus — a $2M deal:

7
00:00:28.931 --> 00:00:38.872
Guy asks: "Is
it like a massive contract with personal

8
00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:42.677
guarantee and all that stuff?".

9
00:00:42.677 --> 00:00:49.077
Andy doesn't just say "no." He validates:
"That's obviously a concern for a lot of

10
00:00:49.077 --> 00:00:55.081
founders." He aligns: "Our founders are
very aligned on that — the pain point

11
00:00:55.081 --> 00:01:01.244
we're looking to solve is really around
digital advertising." Then he gives the

12
00:01:01.244 --> 00:01:07.248
structural answer: "There's no personal
guarantee. We don't do fees. We don't

13
00:01:07.248 --> 00:01:13.095
charge interest. It's a corporate card
issued against your LLC or C corp.".

14
00:01:13.095 --> 00:01:19.175
Three steps. Validate the concern, align
on values, then answer. The prospect

15
00:01:19.175 --> 00:01:22.615
feels heard before they get the
information.

16
00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:27.073
Tactic two: direct answer, then pivot to
simplicity.

17
00:01:27.073 --> 00:01:32.469
Sometimes the best response is the
shortest one — a direct "no" followed by

18
00:01:32.469 --> 00:01:34.875
how easy the process actually is.

19
00:01:34.875 --> 00:01:40.217
Kurt Bell when a prospect asks about
credit checks:

20
00:01:40.217 --> 00:01:46.871
"No personal
guarantee, no credit check, which is a

21
00:01:46.871 --> 00:01:53.151
thing founders appreciate as well.
Application is super lightweight,

22
00:01:53.151 --> 00:02:00.461
probably takes you 10, 15 minutes, just
KYC verification for beneficial owners,

23
00:02:00.461 --> 00:02:05.897
controlling officer, connected bank
account. Very simple.".

24
00:02:05.897 --> 00:02:11.891
Tim Jonas handles the same question:
"We

25
00:02:11.891 --> 00:02:18.869
underwrite completely on the business. No
personal guarantee or personal credit

26
00:02:18.869 --> 00:02:26.116
check for you. For net one, all I need is
you to fill out the application, connect

27
00:02:26.116 --> 00:02:32.200
your business bank account, send me your
EIN document and articles of

28
00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:36.315
organization. That's all we need for net
one.".

29
00:02:36.315 --> 00:02:42.092
Both lead with the direct answer, then
pivot to how simple the next step is. The

30
00:02:42.092 --> 00:02:46.114
concern is addressed and replaced with
forward momentum.

31
00:02:46.114 --> 00:02:50.015
Tactic three: redirect when you hit a
wall.

32
00:02:50.015 --> 00:02:54.506
Sometimes a prospect refuses — not
because of a specific concern, but

33
00:02:54.506 --> 00:02:59.591
because they're not the right person to
share the information. That's when you

34
00:02:59.591 --> 00:03:00.185
redirect.

35
00:03:00.185 --> 00:03:06.791
Cameron Baker on a call with Shop LC — a
$300M company. The prospect, Vijay,

36
00:03:06.791 --> 00:03:12.956
refuses three times to share personal
information for KYC verification:

37
00:03:12.956 --> 00:03:20.920
Vijay: "I
don't want to share my information

38
00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:28.885
because I don't know why my personal
information is being asked here.".

39
00:03:28.885 --> 00:03:33.914
Cameron explains it's for identity
verification and that the data is deleted

40
00:03:33.914 --> 00:03:39.277
if they don't move forward. Vijay refuses
again: "Still, I don't want to share my

41
00:03:39.277 --> 00:03:40.216
information.".

42
00:03:40.216 --> 00:03:45.561
Cameron's next move: "Is there maybe a
business owner that you'd prefer to do

43
00:03:45.561 --> 00:03:47.600
identity verification with?".

44
00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:53.962
Vijay does not complete identity
verification on the call. The credit deal closed at $500K.

45
00:03:53.962 --> 00:03:59.601
When a concern is personal — this
specific person won't share — redirect to

46
00:03:59.601 --> 00:04:05.773
someone who will. When it's structural —
company policy prohibits sharing — accept

47
00:04:05.773 --> 00:04:11.945
it and offer the Net-1 path. Knowing the
difference saves you from pushing against

48
00:04:11.945 --> 00:04:13.698
a wall that won't move.

49
00:04:13.698 --> 00:04:19.306
Not every concern can be resolved. Some
prospects have company policies that

50
00:04:19.306 --> 00:04:25.212
prevent data sharing. Some have portfolio
company governance that requires board

51
00:04:25.212 --> 00:04:30.147
approval. Those are structural —
technique won't overcome them. But

52
00:04:30.147 --> 00:04:35.530
personal concerns — discomfort with
documents, worry about credit checks,

53
00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:41.212
hesitation about personal guarantees —
those are the ones these three tactics

54
00:04:41.212 --> 00:04:42.259
are built for.

55
00:04:42.259 --> 00:04:46.749
The next section shows you how to read
the signals from your discovery

56
00:04:46.749 --> 00:04:51.889
conversation and decide what to do next:
push for float, pivot to Net-1, or flag

57
00:04:51.889 --> 00:04:53.776
it for the underwriting team.
